@page "/workspace"
@inject IBonboardingService BonboardingService
@inject NavigationManager Navigation

<PageTitle>Multi-page tour · Bonboarding</PageTitle>

<div class="bb-intro">
    <h1>Multi-page tour</h1>
    <p class="bb-subtitle">
        Steps can declare the route they belong to. The engine navigates there, waits for the page to render, and
        only then shows the step. If you wander off mid-tour it holds its place instead of dragging you back, and
        picks up where it left off when you return.
    </p>
</div>

<section class="bb-card">
    <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-primary"></span> A tour across two pages</h3>
    <p>
        The tour starts here, crosses to the settings page for two steps and comes back for the last one - the
        navigation is the engine's, not this page's. Things to try:
    </p>
    <ol class="bb-detail-list">
        <li>Run it and press Next: on the third step the engine navigates to the settings page, waits for it to render, and only then looks for the step's target.</li>
        <li>
            While the tour is on a settings step, press the browser's Back button to come back here. The tour holds its
            place with nothing of it left on screen, so the whole site - the docs navigation included - is yours again,
            and it resumes the moment you return to the settings page.
        </li>
        <li>
            Reload the page part way through, then run the tour again: it resumes at the step you were on, because progress
            is stored after every step and <code>StartAsync</code> resumes by default. Finish it and it starts from the top.
        </li>
        <li>Start it from the <a href="workspace/settings">settings page</a> instead: the engine navigates back here for the first step.</li>
    </ol>

    <div class="bb-demo">
        <div class="bb-demo-bar">
            <span class="bb-demo-label">Live</span>
            <button type="button" class="btn btn-primary" @onclick="StartAsync">Run the multi-page tour</button>
            <a class="btn btn-secondary" href="workspace/settings">Go to settings manually</a>
        </div>

        <AppWindow OnSettingsClick="GoToSettings" />
    </div>

    <p>
        The same tour is listed on the <a href="guidance">guidance page</a>'s tour list, which offers to resume it once
        you have left it part way.
    </p>
</section>

<section class="bb-card">
    <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-success"></span> Giving a step a route</h3>
    <p>
        The tour itself is plain data, defined once in C# and started from either page. Give every step of a multi-page
        tour an explicit <code>Id</code>: ids are what is stored, so they are what makes resuming across a reload work.
    </p>
    <ul class="bb-detail-list">
        <li>
            A route is matched against the base-relative path, ignoring case, leading and trailing slashes, the query string and
            the fragment: <code>"/settings/"</code>, <code>"settings"</code> and <code>"settings?tab=keys"</code> are one route.
        </li>
        <li>
            A trailing <code>*</code> makes the route a constraint rather than a destination: the step waits until the user
            is somewhere under it, and the engine does not navigate, since there is no single address to go to.
        </li>
        <li>
            While a step waits for its route, the session is <code>Waiting</code> with <code>WaitReason = Route</code>, and
            nothing is drawn - no overlay, no card - so the page stays fully usable. There is no timeout: a session waiting
            on a route holds no observers and no timers, so waiting costs nothing.
        </li>
        <li>
            <code>AutoNavigateToStepRoute = false</code>, set globally, turns navigation off entirely, so routes are only
            ever constraints.
        </li>
    </ul>

    <pre class="bb-code" tabindex="0">@Example</pre>
</section>

@code {
    private void GoToSettings() => Navigation.NavigateTo("workspace/settings");

    private Task StartAsync() => BonboardingService.StartAsync(WorkspaceTour.Create());

    private const string Example = """
        public static class WorkspaceTour
        {
            public const string Id = "workspace";

            public static BonboardingTourDefinition Create() => new()
            {
                Id = Id,
                Version = 1,   // bump when an edit changes what a saved position means
                Steps =
                {
                    new BonboardingStepDefinition { Id = "overview", Route = "workspace", Target = BonboardingStepTarget.Anchor("metrics"), ... },
                    new BonboardingStepDefinition
                    {
                        Id = "api-keys",
                        Route = "workspace/settings",   // navigate here first, then look for the target
                        Target = BonboardingStepTarget.Anchor("api-keys"),
                        Title = "API keys",
                    },
                    new BonboardingStepDefinition { Id = "back-home", Route = "workspace", ... },   // and back again
                },
            };
        }

        // Both pages:
        <button @onclick="() => BonboardingService.StartAsync(WorkspaceTour.Create())">Run the multi-page tour</button>

        // A constraint rather than a destination: wait until the user is somewhere under /workspace.
        new BonboardingStepDefinition { Route = "workspace/*", ... }

        // Routes as constraints only, everywhere:
        builder.Services.AddBitBonboardingServices(options => options.AutoNavigateToStepRoute = false);
        """;
}
